{"name":"Camellia Rose","hex":"#e94e6d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"camellia","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any plant of the genus Camellia, shrubs and small trees native to Asia; Camellia japonica is the most popular as a garden plant; Camellia sinensis is the tea plant."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/camellia"},{"term":"rose","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shrub of the genus Rosa, with red, pink, white or yellow flowers.","A flower of the rose plant."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make rose-colored; to redden or flush.","To perfume, as with roses."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rose"}],"entries":[{"title":"Camellia","blocks":[{"text":"Camellia is a genus of flowering plants in the family Theaceae. They are found in tropical and subtropical areas in eastern and southern Asia, from the Himalayas east to Japan and Indonesia. There are more than 220 described species; almost all are found in southern China and Indochina. Camellias are popular ornamental, tea, and woody-oil plants cultivated worldwide for centuries. Over 26,000 cultivars, with more than 51,000 cultivar names, including synonyms, have been registered or published."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Camellia_sasanqua1JAM343.jpg/330px-Camellia_sasanqua1JAM343.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camellia","wikidata":"Q212815"},{"title":"Rose","blocks":[{"text":"A rose is either a woody perennial flowering plant of the genus Rosa, in the family Rosaceae, or the flower it bears. There are over three hundred species and tens of thousands of cultivars. The plants range in size and form, from trailing and erect shrubs to climbers up to 7 metres (23 ft) in height. Sharp prickles appear on the stems of many species. Their flowers vary in size and shape and are usually large and showy, in colours ranging from white through pinks, reds, oranges and yellows."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Rosa_rubiginosa_1.jpg/330px-Rosa_rubiginosa_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rose","wikidata":"Q34687"},{"title":"Rose (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Rose is the color halfway between red and magenta, whose hue corresponds to an angle of 330 degrees on the HSV/HSL color wheel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Pink-Rose-Sitia-Crete.jpg/330px-Pink-Rose-Sitia-Crete.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rose_(color)","wikidata":"Q533047"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Camellia_%C3%97_intermedia_fma._alba_%28Tuyama%29_Nagam._in_Fl._Japan_2a-_398_%282006%29_20240216_180127.jpg/500px-Camellia_%C3%97_intermedia_fma._alba_%28Tuyama%29_Nagam._in_Fl._Japan_2a-_398_%282006%29_20240216_180127.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Camellia_%C3%97_intermedia_fma._alba_%28Tuyama%29_Nagam._in_Fl._Japan_2a-_398_%282006%29_20240216_180127.jpg/1280px-Camellia_%C3%97_intermedia_fma._alba_%28Tuyama%29_Nagam._in_Fl._Japan_2a-_398_%282006%29_20240216_180127.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Camellia_%C3%97_intermedia_fma._alba_%28Tuyama%29_Nagam._in_Fl._Japan_2a-_398_%282006%29_20240216_180127.jpg/500px-Camellia_%C3%97_intermedia_fma._alba_%28Tuyama%29_Nagam._in_Fl._Japan_2a-_398_%282006%29_20240216_180127.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Flowering Camellia × intermedia","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACamellia_%C3%97_intermedia_fma._alba_(Tuyama)_Nagam._in_Fl._Japan_2a-_398_(